#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:16
msgid "Abstract"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:18
msgid ""
"Add ``Py_COMPAT_API_VERSION`` C macro that hides some deprecated and soft-"
"deprecated symbols, allowing users to opt out of using API with known issues "
"that other API solves. The macro is versioned, allowing users to update (or "
"not) on their own pace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:23
msgid ""
"Also, add namespaced alternatives for API without the ``Py_`` prefix, and "
"soft-deprecate the original names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:28
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:30
msgid "Some of Python's C API has flaws that are only obvious in hindsight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:32
msgid ""
"If an API prevents adding features or optimizations, or presents a serious "
"security risk or maintenance burden, we can deprecate and remove it as "
"described in :pep:`387`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:36
msgid ""
"However, this leaves us with some API that has “sharp edges” -- it works "
"fine for its current users, but should be avoided in new code. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:40
msgid ""
"API that cannot signal an exception, so failures are either ignored or exit "
"the process with a fatal error. For example ``PyObject_HasAttr``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:42
msgid ""
"API that is not thread-safe, for example by borrowing references from "
"mutable objects, or exposing unfinished mutable objects. For example "
"``PyDict_GetItemWithError``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:45
msgid ""
"API with names that don't use the ``Py``/``_Py`` prefix, and so can clash "
"with other code. For example: ``setter``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:48
msgid ""
"It is important to note that despite such flaws, it's usually possible to "
"use the API correctly. For example, in a single-threaded environment, thread "
"safety is not an issue. We do not want to break working code, even if it "
"uses API that would be wrong in some -- or even *most* -- other contexts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:54
msgid ""
"On the other hand, we want to steer users away from such “undesirable” API "
"in *new* code, especially if a safer alternative exists."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:59 ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:120
msgid "Adding the ``Py`` prefix"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:61
msgid ""
"Some names defined in CPython headers is not namespaced: it that lacks the "
"``Py`` prefix (or a variant: ``_Py``, and alternative capitalizations). For "
"example, we declare a function type named simply ``setter``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:65
msgid ""
"While such names are not exported in the ABI (as checked by ``make "
"smelly``), they can clash with user code and, more importantly, with "
"libraries linked to third-party extensions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:69
msgid ""
"While it would be possible to provide namespaced aliases and "
"(soft-)deprecate these names, the only way to make them not clash with third-"
"party code is to not define them in Python headers at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:75
msgid "Rationale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:77
msgid ""
"We want to allow an easy way for users to avoid “undesirable” API if they "
"choose to do so."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:80
msgid ""
"It might be be sufficient to leave this to third-party linters. For that "
"we'd need a good way to expose a list of (soft-)deprecated API to such "
"linters. While adding that, we can -- rather easily -- do the linter's job "
"directly in CPython headers, avoiding the neel for an extra tool. Unlike "
"Python, C makes it rather easy to limit available API -- for a whole project "
"or for each individual source file -- by having users define an “opt-in” "
"macro."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:89
msgid ""
"We already do something similar with ``Py_LIMITED_API``, which limits the "
"available API to a subset that compiles to stable ABI. (In hindsight, we "
"should have used a different macro name for that particular kind of "
"limiting, but it's too late to change that now.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:94
msgid ""
"To prevent working code from breaking as we identify more “undesirable” API "
"and add safer alternatives to it, the opt-in macro should be *versioned*. "
"Users can choose a version they need based on their compatibility "
"requirements, and update it at their own pace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:99
msgid ""
"To be clear, this mechanism is *not* a replacement for deprecation. "
"Deprecation is for API that prevents new features or optimizations, or "
"presents a security risk or maintenance burden. This mechanism, on the other "
"hand, is meant for cases where “we found a slightly better way of doing "
"things” -- perhaps one that's harder to misuse, or just has a less "
"misleading name. (On a lighter note: many people configure a code quality "
"checker to shout at them about the number of blank lines between functions. "
"Let's help them identify more substantial “code smells”!)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:109
msgid ""
"The proposed macro does not *change* any API definitions; it only *hides* "
"them. So, if code compiles with the macro, it'll also compile without it, "
"with identical behaviour. This has implications for core devs: to deal with "
"undesirable behaviour, we'll need to introduce new, better API, and *then* "
"discourage the old one. In turn, this implies that we should look at an "
"individual API and fix all its known issues at once, rather than do codebase-"
"wide sweeps for a single kind of issue, so that we avoid multiple renames of "
"the same function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:122
msgid ""
"An opt-in macro allows us to omit definitions that could clash with third-"
"party libraries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:127
msgid "Specification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:129
msgid ""
"We introduce a ``Py_COMPAT_API_VERSION`` macro. If this macro is defined "
"before ``#include <Python.h>``, some API definitions -- as described below "
"-- will be omitted from the Python header files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:133
msgid ""
"The macro only omits complete top-level definitions exposed from ``<Python."
"h>``. Other things (the ABI, structure definitions, macro expansions, static "
"inline function bodies, etc.) are not affected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:137
msgid ""
"The C API working group (:pep:`731`) has authority over the set of omitted "
"definitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:140
msgid ""
"The set of omitted definitions will be tied to a particular feature release "
"of CPython, and is finalized in each 3.x.0 Beta 1 release. In rare cases, "
"entries can be removed (i.e. made available for use) at any time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:145
msgid ""
"The macro should be defined to a version in the format used by "
"``PY_VERSION_HEX``, with the “micro”, “release” and “serial” fields set to "
"zero. For example, to omit API deemed undesirable in 3.14.0b1, users should "
"define ``Py_COMPAT_API_VERSION`` to ``0x030e0000``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:153
msgid "Requirements for omitted API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:155
msgid "An API that is omitted with ``Py_COMPAT_API_VERSION`` must:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:157
msgid "be soft-deprecated (see :pep:`387`);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:158
msgid ""
"for all known use cases of the API, have a documented alternative or "
"workaround;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:160
msgid ""
"have tests to ensure it keeps working (except for 1:1 renames using "
"``#define`` or ``typedef``);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:162
msgid ""
"be documented (except if it was never mentioned in previous versions of the "
"documentation); and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:164
msgid ""
"be approved by the C API working group. (The WG may give blanket approvals "
"for groups of related API; see *Initial set* below for examples.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:167
msgid ""
"Note that ``Py_COMPAT_API_VERSION`` is meant for API that can be trivially "
"replaced by a better alternative. API without a replacement should generally "
"be deprecated instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:173
msgid "Location"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:175
msgid ""
"All API definitions omitted by ``Py_COMPAT_API_VERSION`` will be moved to a "
"new header, ``Include/legacy.h``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:178
msgid ""
"This is meant to help linter authors compile lists, so they can flag the API "
"with warnings rather than errors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:181
msgid ""
"Note that for simple renaming of source-only constructs (macros, types), we "
"expect names to be omitted in the same version -- or the same PR -- that "
"adds a replacement. This means that the original definition will be renamed, "
"and a ``typedef`` or ``#define`` for the old name added to ``Include/legacy."
"h``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:189
msgid "Documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:191
msgid "Documentation for omitted API should generally:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:193
msgid "appear after the recommended replacement,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:194
msgid "reference the replacement (e.g. “Similar to X, but…”), and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:195
msgid "focus on differences from the replacement and migration advice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0743.rst:197
msgid "Exceptions are possible if there is a good reason for them."
msgstr ""

